WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.880
Natural selection starts with variants that already exist.

2
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.800
Something in the environment, a predator, a parasite, a rival, a choosy mate, a

3
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.260
drought, makes some of those variants leave more offspring than others.

4
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.020
That difference in reproductive success is selection.

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.480
It happens within one generation.

6
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.180
It becomes evolution only if the variation is inherited, so that the young of the

7
00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.540
survivors resemble them.

8
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.380
And if the environment changes, a different variant can win.

9
00:00:34.383 --> 00:00:35.623
Take the peppered moth.

10
00:00:36.443 --> 00:00:40.003
It comes in a pale form and a dark form, and the colour is inherited.

11
00:00:41.063 --> 00:00:43.063
Birds hunt the moths by sight on tree trunks.

12
00:00:43.763 --> 00:00:48.463
Where pollution killed the lichens and coated the bark with soot, dark moths matched

13
00:00:48.463 --> 00:00:52.463
the bark, and the dark form became common around industrial cities.

14
00:00:53.383 --> 00:00:57.643
When the air cleaned up and the lichens came back, pale moths matched better, and

15
00:00:57.643 --> 00:00:58.643
the dark form declined.

16
00:00:59.743 --> 00:01:00.843
Same moth, same birds.

17
00:01:00.843 --> 00:01:04.023
A different environment favoured a different colour.

18
00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:06.287
How do you show it?

19
00:01:07.127 --> 00:01:12.147
Michael Majerus released live moths of both forms near Cambridge over six years and

20
00:01:12.147 --> 00:01:15.147
recorded which disappeared, presumed eaten by birds.

21
00:01:16.227 --> 00:01:18.047
Cook and colleagues analysed his records.

22
00:01:18.987 --> 00:01:24.067
Each day, a dark moth survived at about 0.91 times the rate of a pale one.

23
00:01:24.827 --> 00:01:27.627
That number is relative, and per day, at one site.

24
00:01:28.427 --> 00:01:32.587
It is a measured fitness component, survival, with a named agent, birds.

25
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.467
Selection also has a shape.

26
00:01:36.447 --> 00:01:37.607
Plot fitness against a trait.

27
00:01:38.547 --> 00:01:41.327
Goldenrod gall flies are caught between two enemies.

28
00:01:42.427 --> 00:01:47.307
Wasps take the larvae in small galls, and birds open the large ones, so middle sized

29
00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:48.567
galls survive best.

30
00:01:49.487 --> 00:01:50.887
That is stabilizing selection.

31
00:01:51.707 --> 00:01:56.587
In crowded lake enclosures, sticklebacks at either extreme of a trait scored better

32
00:01:56.587 --> 00:02:00.007
on stand ins for how many young they could make than fish in the middle.

33
00:02:00.647 --> 00:02:03.647
That is disruptive selection, and crowding made it stronger.

34
00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:07.647
Sometimes the winner depends on how common it is.

35
00:02:08.387 --> 00:02:13.287
In Trinidad streams, researchers made one colour pattern of male guppy rare in some

36
00:02:13.287 --> 00:02:14.967
pools and common in others.

37
00:02:15.967 --> 00:02:19.087
Whichever pattern was rare had better estimated survival.

38
00:02:19.927 --> 00:02:21.627
And selection can act through mating.

39
00:02:22.807 --> 00:02:26.667
Malte Andersson shortened and lengthened the tails of male widowbirds in Kenya.

40
00:02:27.847 --> 00:02:31.907
Males given longer tails attracted more females to nest on their territories.

41
00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:36.830
Each of these studies measured selection within one generation.

42
00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:39.850
None of them, by itself, shows evolution.

43
00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:45.210
For that, the trait must be inherited, so that the next generation carries more of

44
00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:46.210
the favoured variant.

45
00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:48.730
A drought does not create deep beaks.

46
00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.090
It removes more of the shallow ones.

47
00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:56.210
Selection sorts what is already there, and inheritance carries the sorting forward.
